    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d504">

                <pb facs="0953" n="829" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg829"/>

                <head>No. 500.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Señor de
                    Zamacona.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-05-03"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 3,
                        1881</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of the 19th ultimo, relative to the employment of the power of both
                    governments to impart greater security to the inhabitants on the border between
                    the United States and Mexico.</p>
                <p>In reply, I have the honor to state that this government will heartily co-operate
                    for the purpose with that of the Mexican Republic. The case of Captain Gillett
                    of the Texan military service, to which you refer, strongly illustrates the
                    necessity for such co-operation. The act of that officer in abducting from
                    Mexico, without judicial warrant, a person charged with crime seems not to be
                    excusable. This Department has written to the governor of Texas upon the
                    subject, and has suggested that such orders be issued to the commander of their
                    military force as may be practicable to prevent abuses.</p>
                <p>Accept, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES G. BLAINE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
